494
00:39:49,140 --> 00:39:51,580
is bounded by this.

495
00:39:51,580 --> 00:39:53,330
And so that's
really this corner.

496
00:39:57,400 --> 00:39:59,480
The next thing I
want to talk about--

497
00:39:59,480 --> 00:40:02,060
so this is how to
evaluate integrals.

498
00:40:02,060 --> 00:40:05,730
And so there, you have the
fundamental theorem of calculus.

499
00:40:09,020 --> 00:40:12,900
And so we'll give
two versions of that.

500
00:40:12,900 --> 00:40:22,540
So how to evaluate integrals.

501
00:40:27,940 --> 00:40:30,410
And so this is the fundamental
theorem of calculus.

502
00:40:46,250 --> 00:40:48,270
And now, what does the
fundamental theorem

503
00:40:48,270 --> 00:40:51,830
of calculus-- so
let's give version 1.

504
00:40:51,830 --> 00:41:02,350
So version 1-- version 1
is that if you take a--

505
00:41:02,350 --> 00:41:15,990
if you take a function, f,
and f here is continuous,

506
00:41:15,990 --> 00:41:19,830
and you're looking
at the integral--

507
00:41:19,830 --> 00:41:23,470
you're looking at-- so
you have a function.

508
00:41:23,470 --> 00:41:28,950
I don't really think
about it as two versions.

509
00:41:28,950 --> 00:41:36,490
But the version-- but I divided
it into two versions here.

510
00:41:36,490 --> 00:41:40,030
So the function-- if
f is continuous, say,

511
00:41:40,030 --> 00:41:42,810
on the interval from--

512
00:41:42,810 --> 00:41:47,130
on this interval here, and
you define a function, f

513
00:41:47,130 --> 00:41:58,170
of x, which is the integral
from a to x of f of s ds,

514
00:41:58,170 --> 00:42:02,170
you see, for each
fixed x in the interval

515
00:42:02,170 --> 00:42:05,890
here, well, if you're
thinking about the function,

516
00:42:05,890 --> 00:42:10,150
little f, little f was
continuous on the interval.

517
00:42:10,150 --> 00:42:12,630
So it's continuous on--

518
00:42:12,630 --> 00:42:17,690
so f here is continuous on
the interval from a to x.

519
00:42:17,690 --> 00:42:22,010
So when you restrict it,
it's still continuous.

520
00:42:22,010 --> 00:42:24,690
So this means that
it's integrable.

521
00:42:24,690 --> 00:42:28,130
And so this here
gives you a number.

522
00:42:28,130 --> 00:42:31,970
And so, for each x, you can
think about this number here.

523
00:42:31,970 --> 00:42:34,970
So this gives you a
function, capital F.

524
00:42:34,970 --> 00:42:39,150
And the claim is that
this function here--

525
00:42:39,150 --> 00:42:46,190
so the claim is that the
function capital F, defined

526
00:42:46,190 --> 00:42:49,570
this way, is differentiable.

527
00:42:49,570 --> 00:42:52,210
So capital F is differentiable.

528
00:42:57,310 --> 00:43:12,670
And the derivative of this
at x is little f of x.

529
00:43:12,670 --> 00:43:13,950
And maybe I'll just--

530
00:43:13,950 --> 00:43:14,590
yeah.

531
00:43:14,590 --> 00:43:15,900
So that's the claim.

532
00:43:19,670 --> 00:43:21,710
OK.

533
00:43:21,710 --> 00:43:23,010
Let's try to prove that.

534
00:43:30,350 --> 00:43:32,870
So let's look at--

535
00:43:32,870 --> 00:43:34,080
let's try to prove--

536
00:43:37,250 --> 00:43:39,370
so we want to prove--

537
00:43:39,370 --> 00:43:42,700
so we want to prove that this
function-- so we want to prove--

538
00:43:45,650 --> 00:44:04,170
so we want to show that f
prime exists at, say, at x0.

539
00:44:04,170 --> 00:44:07,310
If you want to prove
that it exists,

540
00:44:07,310 --> 00:44:10,437
then you need to look at
the difference quotient.

541
00:44:10,437 --> 00:44:12,520
So we'll need to look at
this difference quotient.

542
00:44:16,330 --> 00:44:18,570
And we need to prove
that this thing here

543
00:44:18,570 --> 00:44:21,350
has a limit as x goes to x0.

544
00:44:21,350 --> 00:44:22,890
So x0 is fixed.

545
00:44:22,890 --> 00:44:25,320
And you want to prove that
it has a limit, right?

546
00:44:31,115 --> 00:44:35,670
So in order to estimate
this, let me just--

547
00:44:35,670 --> 00:44:38,390
let me not divide first.

548
00:44:38,390 --> 00:44:42,430
Let me just look at what
this difference here is.

549
00:44:42,430 --> 00:44:46,630
And then, afterwards, I'm
going to divide by x minus x0.

550
00:44:46,630 --> 00:44:50,510
So if I'm looking at this,
let's, for the sake of argument,

551
00:44:50,510 --> 00:44:54,110
assume that x is bigger than x0.

552
00:44:54,110 --> 00:44:58,470
Of course, I need to also
see when I'm approaching x0

553
00:44:58,470 --> 00:45:00,350
from the other side.

554
00:45:00,350 --> 00:45:03,170
But the argument
for that is similar.

555
00:45:03,170 --> 00:45:09,270
So I just assume that
x is bigger than x0.

556
00:45:09,270 --> 00:45:11,930
Well, this difference here--

557
00:45:11,930 --> 00:45:15,390
well, the function--
so this function here,

558
00:45:15,390 --> 00:45:17,390
the function here-- so
let me just write it over

559
00:45:17,390 --> 00:45:20,910
here so I have a
little more space.

560
00:45:20,910 --> 00:45:23,950
So this difference here,
where the function-- this

561
00:45:23,950 --> 00:45:30,910
here is the integral
from a to x f of s ds.

562
00:45:30,910 --> 00:45:41,210
And this thing here is the
integral from a to x0 f of s ds,

563
00:45:41,210 --> 00:45:43,130
right?

564
00:45:43,130 --> 00:45:49,210
But this first integral here,
I can use this fourth rule

565
00:45:49,210 --> 00:45:51,190
that I maybe don't have anymore.

566
00:45:51,190 --> 00:45:55,970
But the fourth rule is saying
that you can split it up.

567
00:45:55,970 --> 00:45:57,630
An integral, you
can split it up.

568
00:45:57,630 --> 00:46:01,690
So here, if you have
a, then you have x0,

569
00:46:01,690 --> 00:46:06,690
and then you have x, if you want
to integrate little f over this,

570
00:46:06,690 --> 00:46:09,250
it's the same as
integrating over this

571
00:46:09,250 --> 00:46:11,770
plus the integral over that.

572
00:46:11,770 --> 00:46:13,070
So I do that.

573
00:46:13,070 --> 00:46:16,210
So this one here, I
break into two pieces.

574
00:46:16,210 --> 00:46:21,970
I integrate from
a to x0 f of s ds.

575
00:46:21,970 --> 00:46:24,870
And then I take
the second piece,

576
00:46:24,870 --> 00:46:32,230
which is from x0 to x, and
then minus the integral from a

577
00:46:32,230 --> 00:46:36,190
to x0 of f of s ds.

578
00:46:36,190 --> 00:46:38,550
OK?

579
00:46:38,550 --> 00:46:41,430
But now you see
that this one here

580
00:46:41,430 --> 00:46:43,690
cancels out with that, right?

581
00:46:43,690 --> 00:46:51,050
And so this difference is just
the integral from x0 to x f of s

582
00:46:51,050 --> 00:46:51,550
ds.

583
00:46:59,242 --> 00:47:09,190
So what we have is we have
that this difference here,

584
00:47:09,190 --> 00:47:13,830
f of x minus f of x0--

585
00:47:13,830 --> 00:47:19,710
we know that this difference
here is equal to this integral

586
00:47:19,710 --> 00:47:20,210
here.

587
00:47:24,470 --> 00:47:30,690
Now, let me use this to bound
this thing here from above

588
00:47:30,690 --> 00:47:31,920
and bound it from below.

589
00:47:35,890 --> 00:47:39,730
You see, this integral
here, this function here--

590
00:47:39,730 --> 00:47:45,516
so this function here, if you're
looking at this integral here

591
00:47:45,516 --> 00:47:50,650
of f of s ds,
well, this function

592
00:47:50,650 --> 00:47:57,690
here is smaller than the max--

593
00:47:57,690 --> 00:48:00,730
the function is continuous, so
the max and sup is the same--

594
00:48:00,730 --> 00:48:05,210
the max, or the sup, or whatever
you want, on that interval.

595
00:48:05,210 --> 00:48:14,690
So this thing here is less than
the integral of, if you will, x0

596
00:48:14,690 --> 00:48:20,510
to x of the sup of f
on this interval here.

597
00:48:24,690 --> 00:48:27,010
But this is just a constant.

598
00:48:27,010 --> 00:48:29,960
And so this here is just--

599
00:48:29,960 --> 00:48:33,190
this is just like the
sup of the function

600
00:48:33,190 --> 00:48:37,230
on this little interval times--

601
00:48:37,230 --> 00:48:38,770
so this here's a constant.

602
00:48:38,770 --> 00:48:40,590
So this just pops out.

603
00:48:40,590 --> 00:48:43,210
And then it's just the
integral of 1 on this.

604
00:48:43,210 --> 00:48:45,190
But that's just the
length of the interval.

605
00:48:45,190 --> 00:48:48,230
So this is this here times
the length of the interval.

606
00:48:48,230 --> 00:48:53,096
And the length of
the interval is this.

607
00:48:53,096 --> 00:48:58,118
So this thing here
is less than that.

608
00:48:58,118 --> 00:49:02,350
Likewise, this function
here on this little interval

609
00:49:02,350 --> 00:49:05,370
is bigger or equal to the inf.

610
00:49:05,370 --> 00:49:10,550
So this is bigger
than if you were just

611
00:49:10,550 --> 00:49:14,100
integrating the inf of the
function on this same interval.

612
00:49:17,150 --> 00:49:20,750
But the inf-- this
is just a constant.

613
00:49:20,750 --> 00:49:22,190
This pops out.

614
00:49:22,190 --> 00:49:26,410
So this thing here
is just the inf

615
00:49:26,410 --> 00:49:31,530
of the function on
this interval here

616
00:49:31,530 --> 00:49:37,750
times the length of the
interval, like that.

617
00:49:40,876 --> 00:49:44,210
So now I can-- and remember
that this thing here

618
00:49:44,210 --> 00:49:46,690
was equal to the difference.

619
00:49:46,690 --> 00:49:48,790
So now I can just record this.

620
00:49:48,790 --> 00:49:52,690
So what I have is now--

621
00:49:52,690 --> 00:49:58,330
what I'm interested in is in
the difference in capital F.

622
00:49:58,330 --> 00:50:01,230
So what I'm interested in is--

623
00:50:01,230 --> 00:50:04,610
of course, I really want to
look at the difference quotient.

624
00:50:04,610 --> 00:50:06,590
But I can do this
in just a second.

625
00:50:06,590 --> 00:50:10,930
So I'm interested in the
difference of the function

626
00:50:10,930 --> 00:50:13,390
at those two points.

627
00:50:13,390 --> 00:50:18,290
I know that that difference is
equal to that integral here.

628
00:50:18,290 --> 00:50:23,130
And that integral is bounded
from above by this and below

629
00:50:23,130 --> 00:50:24,010
by that.

630
00:50:24,010 --> 00:50:25,170
So I just write--

631
00:50:25,170 --> 00:50:28,250
I can now just write that
this difference here--

632
00:50:28,250 --> 00:50:31,030
maybe I should write it so
I have a little more space.

633
00:50:31,030 --> 00:50:36,470
Somehow, F of x minus
F of x0, the difference

634
00:50:36,470 --> 00:50:42,990
is bounded from above by this
thing here, the sup of f,

635
00:50:42,990 --> 00:50:48,430
on this little interval,
times x minus x0.

636
00:50:48,430 --> 00:50:55,110
And it's bounded
from below by the inf

637
00:50:55,110 --> 00:51:02,430
on that same interval times
the length of the interval.

638
00:51:06,110 --> 00:51:08,630
So now we can go to
the difference quotient

639
00:51:08,630 --> 00:51:12,030
because we just divide by
x minus x0 on both sides.

640
00:51:12,030 --> 00:51:14,750
x is bigger than x0.

641
00:51:14,750 --> 00:51:17,430
So you have this thing here.

642
00:51:17,430 --> 00:51:19,490
So you have this
difference quotient here.

643
00:51:19,490 --> 00:51:22,890
And that's what we want
to show has a limit.

644
00:51:22,890 --> 00:51:26,010
And now we have that
this is less than sup

645
00:51:26,010 --> 00:51:30,290
of f on this little
interval here.

646
00:51:30,290 --> 00:51:34,450
And it's bigger or
equal to the inf of f

647
00:51:34,450 --> 00:51:35,920
on that same little interval.

648
00:51:39,520 --> 00:51:43,650
But you see that as x here--

649
00:51:43,650 --> 00:51:44,560
but now you have--

650
00:51:48,930 --> 00:51:51,680
now you have that
as x goes to x0--

651
00:52:02,790 --> 00:52:11,130
so now, as x goes
to x0, the values--

652
00:52:11,130 --> 00:52:15,570
and not just the value at x, but
actually, in this whole interval

653
00:52:15,570 --> 00:52:16,210
here--

654
00:52:16,210 --> 00:52:20,970
the values goes to what
the values is at x0.

655
00:52:20,970 --> 00:52:26,070
So you see that this here,
the right-hand side here, goes

656
00:52:26,070 --> 00:52:29,190
to f of x-- little f of x0.

657
00:52:29,190 --> 00:52:34,430
And the left-hand side
goes to little f of x0.

658
00:52:34,430 --> 00:52:37,130
So this means that this thing
is squeezed between these two

659
00:52:37,130 --> 00:52:37,630
numbers.

660
00:52:37,630 --> 00:52:39,210
That goes to the same number.

661
00:52:39,210 --> 00:52:42,290
So this difference
quotient also goes--

662
00:52:42,290 --> 00:52:47,950
so this difference
quotient here goes

663
00:52:47,950 --> 00:52:54,190
to f of x0, because
it's squeezed

664
00:52:54,190 --> 00:52:59,150
between those two numbers that
converge to the same number--

665
00:52:59,150 --> 00:53:03,470
namely, to f of x--
little f of x0, right?

666
00:53:03,470 --> 00:53:08,630
So this here proves that it's--
but technically speaking,

667
00:53:08,630 --> 00:53:13,950
I only did it where I'm assuming
that x was bigger than x0.

668
00:53:13,950 --> 00:53:18,570
If you let-- so you
also need to check

669
00:53:18,570 --> 00:53:21,290
when x is strictly
less than x0, and it's

670
00:53:21,290 --> 00:53:25,010
converging to x0 from below.

671
00:53:25,010 --> 00:53:30,240
But with trivial modification,
the same proof works.

672
00:53:30,240 --> 00:53:32,830
And that's done
out in the notes.

673
00:53:32,830 --> 00:53:34,920
So I will not go through that.

674
00:53:38,430 --> 00:53:40,150
Now let's talk about--

675
00:53:40,150 --> 00:53:43,970
so this is the second version
of the fundamental theorem

676
00:53:43,970 --> 00:53:44,500
of calculus.

677
00:53:51,810 --> 00:53:55,890
And so-- again,
you wouldn't really

678
00:53:55,890 --> 00:53:58,250
eventually think
about it, probably,

679
00:53:58,250 --> 00:54:00,330
as two different versions.

680
00:54:00,330 --> 00:54:07,410
But they're slightly-- it's two
things about the same stuff.

681
00:54:07,410 --> 00:54:09,510
But the second version here--

682
00:54:09,510 --> 00:54:11,670
So I divided it into
these two versions.

683
00:54:11,670 --> 00:54:14,810
So the second version--

684
00:54:14,810 --> 00:54:26,650
so again, fundamental theorem
of calculus, version 2.

685
00:54:30,230 --> 00:54:32,950
And so this is the following--

686
00:54:32,950 --> 00:54:37,790
that you take a function,
F, that is defined--

687
00:54:37,790 --> 00:54:46,950
and F is defined on some
interval from a to b into R.

688
00:54:46,950 --> 00:54:50,510
And F is differentiable.

689
00:54:50,510 --> 00:54:53,350
So this here exists.

690
00:54:53,350 --> 00:54:57,070
It's differentiable
at each point.

691
00:54:57,070 --> 00:55:04,330
And f here, this little
f, is Riemann integrable.

692
00:55:09,630 --> 00:55:19,050
And then the claim is that
F of b minus capital F at a

693
00:55:19,050 --> 00:55:28,790
is the integral from a
to b of little f of x dx.

694
00:55:28,790 --> 00:55:29,770
So that's the claim.

695
00:55:40,422 --> 00:55:45,870
I wonder if I should
just skip that.

696
00:55:45,870 --> 00:55:50,270
I did the proof for this
out in the notes also.

697
00:55:50,270 --> 00:55:52,000
So maybe I'll just skip that.

698
00:55:56,730 --> 00:56:04,930
And then-- OK, so now-- but
let me look at an example

699
00:56:04,930 --> 00:56:06,670
where I'm applying it.

700
00:56:19,870 --> 00:56:21,710
And so I have--

701
00:56:21,710 --> 00:56:26,550
I'm looking at-- so
suppose I want to compute

702
00:56:26,550 --> 00:56:30,910
x squared from 0 to 1 dx.

703
00:56:30,910 --> 00:56:34,710
Suppose I want to compute that.

704
00:56:34,710 --> 00:56:37,370
And so, in this case,
I could look at--

705
00:56:37,370 --> 00:56:40,350
so if I want to
apply that version,

706
00:56:40,350 --> 00:56:42,070
I could think
about-- suppose I'm

707
00:56:42,070 --> 00:56:49,256
looking at F of x equal to
x to the power 3 over 3.

708
00:56:49,256 --> 00:56:51,590
Suppose I'm looking at this.

709
00:56:51,590 --> 00:56:54,310
Well, if I'm looking
at this function,

710
00:56:54,310 --> 00:56:59,630
I know that capital F of x is
equal to the derivative of this.

711
00:56:59,630 --> 00:57:01,550
It's just x squared.

712
00:57:01,550 --> 00:57:02,670
Right.

713
00:57:02,670 --> 00:57:05,950
So this, say-- and x
squared is now-- of course,

714
00:57:05,950 --> 00:57:07,130
it's continuous.

715
00:57:07,130 --> 00:57:09,110
So it's integrable.

716
00:57:09,110 --> 00:57:13,610
So this means that
by the second--

717
00:57:13,610 --> 00:57:34,610
by this second version of the
fundamental theorem of calculus,

718
00:57:34,610 --> 00:57:42,290
then we have that
F of 1 minus F of 0

719
00:57:42,290 --> 00:57:47,470
is equal to the integral
of the derivative,

720
00:57:47,470 --> 00:57:51,770
because the derivative
is integrable, so 0 to 1,

721
00:57:51,770 --> 00:57:57,170
of x squared dx.

722
00:57:57,170 --> 00:58:01,810
But this here is now just
evaluating this function here.

723
00:58:01,810 --> 00:58:06,010
So this here is 1/3 minus 0.

724
00:58:06,010 --> 00:58:08,650
So this original
integral is 1/3.

725
00:58:13,035 --> 00:58:13,535
OK.

726
00:58:16,750 --> 00:58:19,990
So the next thing--

727
00:58:19,990 --> 00:58:28,330
so the next thing I want to talk
about is improper integrals.

728
00:58:28,330 --> 00:58:30,690
So this here
concludes-- this here

729
00:58:30,690 --> 00:58:33,230
was stuff I actually had
from the previous lecture.

730
00:58:33,230 --> 00:58:35,450
That was why I skipped
a couple of things.

731
00:58:39,630 --> 00:58:43,470
But yeah, but the details
for the things I skipped

732
00:58:43,470 --> 00:58:45,010
is in the notes.

733
00:58:45,010 --> 00:58:47,430
And the things I skipped
is very similar to things

734
00:58:47,430 --> 00:58:49,040
I went through here, anyway.

735
00:58:53,030 --> 00:58:56,470
So now, let me look
at improper integrals.

736
00:58:56,470 --> 00:59:00,310
And there is a couple
of versions of this.

737
00:59:00,310 --> 00:59:02,910
So we will look at
two versions of this.

738
00:59:02,910 --> 00:59:05,590
One is where the integral--

739
00:59:05,590 --> 00:59:07,250
where the integral is--

